From a30028f3e68d5035e67ca79c5ed93c931f43a572 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 20:03:52 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] wmr: log the controller's factory calibration, and find the
 IMU-to-LED-model bridge in it

wmr_controller_base.c has carried a note since 2026-08-12 saying an IMU-backed
prior on the constellation solve is "NOT a drop-in", because the solve's
orientation and the IMU fusion's live in different frames -- the LED model in
the controller's calibration frame, the fusion in the IMU's own -- and that
resolving that fixed transform from the factory calibration is what such a
prior needs first. Measured then: the gravity axis alone disagrees by 104-161
degrees, bimodally, matching the two position clusters of a bistable solve.

That transform turns out to be parsed already and sitting unused. The
controller config carries both the LED model and the inertial sensors from the
same CalibrationInformation block, so `sensors.accel.pose` is the bridge. This
logs it once per controller at parse time; identity would have meant the data
is genuinely absent, so the line answers the question either way.

Read off two real G2 controllers:

    left  32 LEDs | accel pose p=( 0.0342 -0.0777 0.0031) q=(0.3573  0.6183 -0.3495 0.6066)
    right 32 LEDs | accel pose p=(-0.0328 -0.0767 0.0025) q=(0.3573 -0.6228  0.3440 0.6050)

Not identity: 105.3 and 105.5 degrees of rotation, 85 and 83 mm of offset, and
the two are mirror images of each other as left/right should be. And 105.3
degrees is the LOW MODE of the 104-161 degree disagreement measured before --
within a degree.

So the blocker's premise is answered, and the bimodality gets an explanation
with a discriminator attached: apply the transform and the correct pose's
gravity-axis disagreement should collapse toward zero while the wrong one keeps
the remaining ~161, which is exactly the outside information needed to break a
tie that no threshold on the tracker's own metrics can (measured 2026-08-13:
~20 cm of scatter between solutions for a motionless controller, each reporting
an excellent 0.17 px reprojection error).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_config.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_config.c b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_config.c
index 90957271c..80a0812ad 100644
--- a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_config.c
+++ b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_config.c
@@ -589,6 +589,23 @@ wmr_controller_config_parse(struct wmr_controller_config *c, char *json_string,
 		c->led_count++;
 	}
 
+	// Does the factory calibration actually carry the IMU's pose inside the same frame the
+	// LED model lives in? That transform is the missing piece for an IMU-backed prior on the
+	// constellation solve -- the solve is bistable (measured 2026-08-13: ~20 cm of scatter
+	// between solutions for a motionless controller, each reporting an excellent 0.17 px
+	// reprojection error), no threshold on its own metrics can separate the two poses, and
+	// the IMU is the only independent evidence available. It was blocked on "the two live in
+	// different frames"; this line answers whether the bridge is already parsed and sitting
+	// unused, or genuinely absent. Identity here means absent.
+	WMR_INFO(log_level,
+	         "controller calib: %d LEDs | accel pose p=(%.4f %.4f %.4f) q=(%.4f %.4f %.4f %.4f) | "
+	         "gyro pose p=(%.4f %.4f %.4f)",
+	         c->led_count, c->sensors.accel.pose.position.x, c->sensors.accel.pose.position.y,
+	         c->sensors.accel.pose.position.z, c->sensors.accel.pose.orientation.x,
+	         c->sensors.accel.pose.orientation.y, c->sensors.accel.pose.orientation.z,
+	         c->sensors.accel.pose.orientation.w, c->sensors.gyro.pose.position.x,
+	         c->sensors.gyro.pose.position.y, c->sensors.gyro.pose.position.z);
+
 	cJSON_Delete(json_root);
 
 	return true;
-- 
